# Aux environment variables
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in. Every key is prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ because
# Expo inlines them into the client bundle at build time — treat all of them as
# publicly readable and NEVER put a service-role key or client secret here.
# Restart the dev server after editing; these are baked in, not read at runtime.

# Supabase project URL. Supabase Dashboard > Project Settings > Data API > Project URL.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# Supabase anon/publishable key — safe to ship, RLS is what protects the data.
# Supabase Dashboard > Project Settings > API Keys > anon public.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Spotify app Client ID for PKCE auth. developer.spotify.com/dashboard > your app
# > Settings > Client ID. Register BOTH redirect URIs there too, exactly as written:
# `aux://spotify-callback` (native) and `<origin>/spotify-callback` for every origin
# you serve the web build from, e.g. `http://localhost:8081/spotify-callback`.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=your-spotify-client-id

# The YouTube Data API key is deliberately NOT here, and must never be an
# EXPO_PUBLIC_ variable. It is a BILLING credential with no per-user scoping —
# 10,000 quota units/day against the owner's Google Cloud project, 100 per search
# — and every EXPO_PUBLIC_ value is inlined into the shipped bundle, where it is
# trivially read out of an APK or the web build. It lives only in an Edge
# Function secret, which `search-tracks` and `resolve-track` read server-side:
#   npx supabase secrets set YOUTUBE_API_KEY=your-youtube-api-key
# See docs/SETUP.md step 5.
